s, Q of Diamonds, 10 of Clubs, 8 of Hearts.\n\n10: High Card: No made hand; highest single card wins. \n\nNote for Action representation: -1 for Fold, -2 for All-in. 0 for Call/Check. >0 for the amount you raise the bet. \n\nWhen placing a raise, the chips bet must be at least twice the current maximum bet of this round. If no player has bet before in this round, the raise must be at least equal to the big blind.\n\nTo call or raise, a player must have enough chips to cover the required bet. Otherwise, they can only fold or go all-in. An improper bet is considered an illegal action and will be treated as a fold.\n\nNow is your turn, to your best ability, please select an action from your valid actions that maximize your probability of winning. If you have more chips than your opponent by the last hand of the game, you win the game.\nPlease briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n    Your response format should be:\n    Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n    Reason: [Your Reason]\n    Answer: [Your Answer] (should be a number)\n", 'user_prompt': 'This is the 15 hand out of 20 hands. Everyone starts with 20,000 chips each hand, and you have 19950. Your cumulative win/loss on chips until last hand are -850.0, but the cumulative win/loss chips are inaccessible and independent from your current chips (consider them as deposits/debts to a bank).You are currently losing to your opponent.\nYour cards are: Ace of Clubs, 5 of Hearts.\nCurrently there is no public card shown.\nThe Game history is as follows: \nYour valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 200 (raise the bet), or any value above 200 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '**Analyzing the Hand and Formulating a Strategy**\n\nOkay, so I\'m in hand 15 of 20, down 850 chips. My cards are Ace of Clubs, 5 of Hearts – A5 offsuit. I\'m playing heads-up, which changes the game. I have 19,950 chips, so I\'m assuming I posted the small blind (50). The big blind is 100. The history is empty, meaning no one\'s bet yet.\n\nThe prompts\' provided valid actions are a little confusing, but based on typical poker rules and the starting chip counts, I think I\'m the small blind and the opponent is the big blind. The "0 (Check)" option is weird, as SB usually can\'t check pre-flop unless the BB folded or something weird is happening; I\'ll assume it means call. If 0 means to match and 200 is to raise 200, 200 is my move.\n\nGiven the hand and the situation, A5 offsuit is decent in heads-up. Folding is too tight. Limping (0) is okay but raising (200) seems better. I\'m down and need to be aggressive. A raise creates fold equity. If the opponent folds, I win the blinds. If they call, I have position after the flop, which is important. Raising is a good option.\n\nThe goal is to accumulate chips and A5 is a strong hand for this. I\'m going to raise to 200.\n\n**Final Decision: Raise to 200**\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: A5 offsuit is a respectable starting hand in heads-up play, typically ranking within the top 45% of hands. It possesses high card strength with the Ace and potential for a straight (the wheel). Since you are currently losing and approaching the end of the game (Hand 15/20), playing aggressively to seize initiative and potentially steal the blinds is strategically sound. Folding is too conservative for an Ace in heads-up, and checking/limping gives the opponent a free look at the flop.\n\nReason: Raising to 200 (a standard opening raise) puts pressure on the opponent, potentially taking down the pot pre-flop. If called, you retain the initiative and position, allowing you to represent a strong range on the flop. This maximizes the expected value of the hand compared to a passive call or fold.\n\nAnswer: 200'}
